1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,152
Open A09 and A10 side by side, and give this the same twenty minutes every workshop

2
00:00:05,152 --> 00:00:06,830
in this course has asked for.

3
00:00:06,830 --> 00:00:08,277
The bar is identical too.

4
00:00:08,277 --> 00:00:08,971
Not perfect.

5
00:00:08,971 --> 00:00:09,492
Complete.

6
00:00:09,492 --> 00:00:14,063
Every number filled in with something, even where that something is an estimate

7
00:00:14,063 --> 00:00:18,576
you'll refine later, the same move as Section three, five, and six's workshops

8
00:00:18,576 --> 00:00:19,502
before this one.

9
00:00:19,502 --> 00:00:24,188
Cost and quality live on completely different spreadsheets in most companies, and

10
00:00:24,188 --> 00:00:27,197
latency lives on a dashboard neither one ever opens.

11
00:00:27,197 --> 00:00:31,942
This workshop's entire point is putting all three on one page, for one feature, so

12
00:00:31,942 --> 00:00:36,686
the trade-offs this section named stop being abstract and start being a number you

13
00:00:36,686 --> 00:00:37,901
can defend in a room.

14
00:00:37,901 --> 00:00:40,910
That room is usually closer than it feels right now.

15
00:00:40,910 --> 00:00:45,481
A pricing review, a roadmap prioritisation meeting, a CFO asking what a feature

16
00:00:45,481 --> 00:00:49,936
actually costs to run at scale: this model is the document you'd want in hand

17
00:00:49,936 --> 00:00:54,738
walking into any of those, built once instead of assembled under pressure the night

18
00:00:54,738 --> 00:00:55,143
before.

19
00:00:55,693 --> 00:01:00,419
Notice the first block leans on two lectures at once, because cost never stands

20
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:00,778
alone.

21
00:01:00,778 --> 00:01:05,563
7.1 gave you the billing shape, tokens in and tokens out, and 7.2 turned it into

22
00:01:05,563 --> 00:01:06,520
your own number.

23
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:11,306
If you already have A09 filled in from that lecture, this block is a five-minute

24
00:01:11,306 --> 00:01:12,502
copy, not a rebuild.

25
00:01:12,502 --> 00:01:16,629
The second block is where most people find the workshop's real value.

26
00:01:16,629 --> 00:01:21,534
Running your own numbers through 7.4's floor first, before cost enters the picture

27
00:01:21,534 --> 00:01:26,499
at all, usually disqualifies a candidate someone was quietly already leaning toward

28
00:01:26,499 --> 00:01:27,696
for its price alone.

29
00:01:27,696 --> 00:01:30,627
The third block moves fastest for most proposals.

30
00:01:30,627 --> 00:01:35,472
Check your traffic against caching, batching, and routing's "earns its cost when"

31
00:01:35,472 --> 00:01:40,497
column from 7.3, and if nothing matches yet, write down "none of these yet" and move

32
00:01:40,497 --> 00:01:40,676
on.

33
00:01:40,676 --> 00:01:44,684
That's a legitimate finding, the same honesty 7.3 itself asked for.

34
00:01:44,684 --> 00:01:48,452
What's the exact tier where your feature's margin crosses zero?

35
00:01:48,452 --> 00:01:52,400
Minutes twelve through sixteen ask for that number, not a feeling.

36
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:57,186
If you don't know your heaviest real user's usage, that's the finding this block

37
00:01:57,186 --> 00:02:00,356
exists to produce, the same way it was in 7.5 itself.

38
00:02:00,356 --> 00:02:03,048
The last block is the newest tool in the kit.

39
00:02:03,048 --> 00:02:07,893
Name your interaction pattern from 7.6's table, read off its real tolerance, then

40
00:02:07,893 --> 00:02:11,003
check whether today's build actually fits inside it.

41
00:02:11,553 --> 00:02:12,687
Recognise this shape.

42
00:02:12,687 --> 00:02:17,169
It's 7.4's own worked example of a decision written down properly, and it's roughly

43
00:02:17,169 --> 00:02:21,651
what your workshop's final line should sound like once all five blocks are actually

44
00:02:21,651 --> 00:02:22,191
filled in.

45
00:02:22,191 --> 00:02:26,726
Notice what the sentence contains: a choice, a number, and the reason the number was

46
00:02:26,726 --> 00:02:27,428
worth paying.

47
00:02:27,428 --> 00:02:31,910
Notice what it leaves out: no apology, no hedge, no "we'll revisit this eventually"

48
00:02:31,910 --> 00:02:34,286
doing the work an actual decision should do.

49
00:02:34,286 --> 00:02:38,498
That's the standard this workshop is asking you to hit, not just for the model

50
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:42,008
choice, but for the pricing and latency lines sitting next to it.

51
00:02:42,008 --> 00:02:46,057
Compare it against whatever you'd have written before this section started.

52
00:02:46,057 --> 00:02:50,323
Most first attempts at justifying a model or a price point sound like "flagship

53
00:02:50,323 --> 00:02:54,859
seemed like the safer choice," a sentence with no number in it anyone could actually

54
00:02:54,859 --> 00:02:55,453
argue with.

55
00:02:55,453 --> 00:02:58,801
Twenty minutes from now, yours should read closer to this one.

56
00:02:59,351 --> 00:03:03,849
Say the honest caveat, because minute twelve is where people quietly stop being

57
00:03:03,849 --> 00:03:06,354
rigorous, exactly where rigour matters most.

58
00:03:06,354 --> 00:03:10,966
If your heaviest real user's actual usage produces a margin number that makes you

59
00:03:10,966 --> 00:03:13,016
wince, that's not the model failing.

60
00:03:13,016 --> 00:03:17,628
That's the model doing its job before a launch instead of during a finance review

61
00:03:17,628 --> 00:03:20,076
that catches you off guard a quarter later.

62
00:03:20,076 --> 00:03:24,745
Write the real tier down, then decide what to do about it: a usage-based add-on, a

63
00:03:24,745 --> 00:03:29,528
generous cap, or accepting the loss deliberately because the tier is rare enough not

64
00:03:29,528 --> 00:03:30,097
to matter.

65
00:03:30,097 --> 00:03:33,741
The same discipline applies to the latency block right after it.

66
00:03:33,741 --> 00:03:38,296
It's tempting to write down today's measured number and call the budget question

67
00:03:38,296 --> 00:03:38,809
answered.

68
00:03:38,809 --> 00:03:43,591
Set the budget from the interaction first instead, the way 7.6 argued, and only then

69
00:03:43,591 --> 00:03:48,374
check whether today's build actually fits inside it, rather than defining the budget

70
00:03:48,374 --> 00:03:51,392
as whatever the current build happens to already hit.

71
00:03:51,942 --> 00:03:56,439
That's today's bar, stated as plainly as it was in every workshop before this one:

72
00:03:56,439 --> 00:04:01,046
attempted, not perfected, and the one number most likely to be quietly softened, the

73
00:04:01,046 --> 00:04:02,747
margin tier, computed for real.

74
00:04:02,747 --> 00:04:05,489
If you finish with time left, don't polish evenly.

75
00:04:05,489 --> 00:04:09,876
Return to the margin block specifically, the same instruction as always, because

76
00:04:09,876 --> 00:04:14,483
it's the block most likely to be quietly incomplete or rounded toward comfort rather

77
00:04:14,483 --> 00:04:15,251
than measured.

78
00:04:15,251 --> 00:04:19,749
Resist the urge to smooth the cost and quality blocks instead, even though they're

79
00:04:19,749 --> 00:04:23,039
the ones with the most satisfying numbers already filled in.

80
00:04:23,039 --> 00:04:27,591
A tidy scorecard sitting next to an unchecked margin question is a model that looks

81
00:04:27,591 --> 00:04:31,979
more finished than it actually is, and looking finished is exactly the trap this

82
00:04:31,979 --> 00:04:33,899
course keeps warning you away from.

83
00:04:34,449 --> 00:04:36,008
One thing before you move on.

84
00:04:36,008 --> 00:04:37,244
Finish all five blocks.

85
00:04:37,244 --> 00:04:41,707
Anywhere the honest answer is an estimate, label it an estimate rather than letting

86
00:04:41,707 --> 00:04:43,588
it read as more certain than it is.

87
00:04:43,588 --> 00:04:48,050
Then send it to whoever actually owns pricing for this feature, today, and ask what

88
00:04:48,050 --> 00:04:49,502
number surprises them most.

89
00:04:49,502 --> 00:04:52,459
Expect it to be the margin tier, not the cost per call.

90
00:04:52,459 --> 00:04:56,867
That question alone is often worth more than the twenty minutes you spent building

91
00:04:56,867 --> 00:04:57,405
the model.

92
00:04:57,405 --> 00:05:01,921
It's the same finding this course keeps returning to: the person closest to a number

93
00:05:01,921 --> 00:05:05,953
sees the gap in minutes that would take you a week to notice working alone.

94
00:05:05,953 --> 00:05:10,308
Next lecture closes this section: what these six lectures actually built, and how

95
00:05:10,308 --> 00:05:14,824
directly it plugs into risk and governance next, where a feature's blast radius gets

96
00:05:14,824 --> 00:05:17,835
weighed against exactly the economics you just modelled.
